  volume    = {1},
  number    = {1},
  pages     = {72--87},
  month     = may,
  abstract  = {A model is proposed to study the effects of linkage of two loci, at each of which two alleles are possible. In a finite, random-mating monoecious population, it will usually be the case that one locus will fix first, followed by fixation of the other locus. The probabilities for which locus will fix first, which alleles will become fixed, and the rates at which these fixations occur, are found in terms of the recombination fraction for the pair of loci in question, and the initial state of the population. Most of the results are at least qualitatively similar to those found by Karlin and McGregor (1968) (Genetics 58, 141159) for a different model, but fixation of the first locus is found to be quickest with tight but not complete linkage, in contrast with their conclusion that looser linkage speeds first-fixation.},
